1. Current ᚼ operator hole identified: **physical `reference_direction` is missing**.

Should just be positive or negative for all rotations and expansions. There are a number of ways each could be calculated. But the ideal would be : calculate the parameters of a line with twist evolving into a helix; use SAT bending calculations or assign an arbitrary 'unit twist/coil' relationship. When a straight tube twists, it exhibits predictable coiling. As soon as a distinct curvature radius is apparent, the index sphere for that coil 'emerges' (read: can be measured to define an index sphere). Retain that sphere, and replace the straight tube with a coiled helical tube. Treat it the same way (if you can determin whether a coil will exhibit greater or lesser twist deformation, you can adjust accordingly, but I would suppose a coil is "floppier" than a tube). Let it evolve until you get enough of a second-order helical coil to measure its circumference and assign that to the second index sphere. The important figure will be the value of hagalaz when the loop reaches closure. Now, the first and second order helices have a precise relationship measured by the transformation from the first to the second sphere. It's rotation, expansion, and displacement simultaneously, so integrate I would think. And, actually, look at the geometry to see if we even need to specify displacement, or if that is emergent from rotation and expansion alone. I suspect so.&#x20;





Now, the part I meant to get to when I was first spelling this out is this: Take the three spheres (and you should have new spheres content int he recent uploads)... Basically, a sphere with a second centered on its surface, and a third centered on that (probably with an inherent scaling, but could be represented as three unit spheres I suppose--whether we get distorted paths or not would tell us what/whether there's a scale factor--and just like hagalaz, this is another repurpose of the UI. Now, define a reconnection parameter. We know we're going to be modeling helices, so take this configuration of spheres and ask... what's most naturally helical aspect of the system? Or better, go with consistency: Find the helix here that best fits how we represent it with hagalaz. But picture intersections of the spheres as something like (or exactly like) a current loop closure Three spheres gives you two circular intersections (in 3D)... now let them wobble, maybe compress or intersect one another farther [and no, this isn't ontology, this is scaffoldig for th helix]. What contitions obtain when we apply the hagalaz operator as the defining transformation from one sphere into the adjacent. Can you get a helical particle worldline out of this geometry?&#x20;



Now\... let's imagine the different dynamics that could happen... the three spheres really only being a selection of three adjacent spheres in, probably something like a 24-cell lattice. So, yo watch the charge "reach" out as a current makes circuit closure. Wobble / disotrion maybe allows propogation from one "saddle" interesection to the next... but in this system, what happens if an electrical cuurent traveling through this matrix encounters a barrier or pushback? What do helices do when they have too much slack to stretch to where they were headed. What does electrcity do? Well, helices can overcoil if they're given too much twist, or flip orientation plane if a kink or reorientation of twist/coil direction happens. Now\... there's a lot to be reconciled, and worldtube is not currently identified directly with electrical curent per se, and electrogravacoustics (search, but I think ther's something to these relationships.

Oscillations are oscillations -- in any orientation at any scale, made of any components... we just need to keep careful track of what the base rule is, what scales and how the scales differ. BUt in general it's easiest to start by using what you can figure out about one scale to apply a first order coarse graining (say, fundamental filament coils to planetary orbits, or vice versa) ...gravity does not behave like electricity... except it kind of does. If you cancel out all the chirality and change the proportional worldtube bending and medium response to differ.... how do Kelvin vortices behave at planetary scales and timespans and is there a superfluid medium or a viscous one? These change how things behave.&#x20;

BUt the fundamental SAT procedure *always* applies. 1) Map what you know in 4D using standard physics and the primitives you ahve at your disposal (in SAT that's a line and a plane and thats it--although we did eventually have to add some kind fo mediaum) 2) Map it in as much detail as yo ucan for the most well constrained systms we can identify 3) Throw the labels out the window, and look at that map. *It* canot be wrong if you stuck to well constrained systems and you did your dimensinal analysis right. It is a map. Pick a system. Map it. Look at it. Pick a minimally different system map it look at it. What's different?&#x20;

Rinse. Wash. Repeat. Until you've got your question space narrowed down enough to make some guess and go throough the possibilities like tiral anderror. And if you can't figure it out... go to the next derivation and come back. SAT could calculae from bernouli up to the lagrangian, back down to neutron star dynamics, back up the derivation chain, back down a different branch... and it could close the loop, furher constraining with each pass, using the constants as the torque wrenches. Look at any of the "one drop", "standalone", "altogether" or other similarly named big files in the SAT archive. Those are your map. Those are your stick figures. All you're doing is shading in the bulk.
